<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Piraeus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Piraeus is the port city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, about ten kilometres south-west of the centre. Fortified on the initiative of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> in the 490s and 480s BCE and laid out on a grid plan by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippodamus-of-miletus">Hippodamus</a>, it was the base of the Athenian navy and the city's grain trade, and from about 457 BCE was joined to Athens by the Long Walls. It is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>'s largest port.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piraeus.jpg" alt="Piraeus" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pitichinaccio · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2011_Dimos_Pireos.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
